      "fulltextExcerpts": [
        "To justify the approximation, we make four assumptions. We verify its accuracy empirically in Section 7.\n\nAssumption 1 Observe that the stochastic gradient is a sum of S independent, uniformly sampled contributions. Invoking the central limit theorem, we assume that the gradient noise is Gaussian with covariance 1 S C(θ), hence\n\nAssumption 2 We assume that the covariance matrix C(θ) is approximately constant with respect to θ.",
        "In addition to Assumptions 1-4 of Section 3, we need an additional assumption for our theoretical considerations to hold: Assumption 5 Assume that the sample size N is large enough that the Bernstein-von Mises theorem (Le Cam, 1986) applies (hence the posterior is Gaussian). Also assume that the observed dataset was drawn from the model p(y |θ) with parameter θ = 0. Then A = BB , that is, the Fisher information matrix equals the Hessian.",
        "It is hard to say in general how small S needs to be to satisfy assumptions 1-4. But if assumption 4 is satisfied (i.e., the cost is approximately quadratic), then assumption 3 (no discretization error) cannot hold if > 2 λmax . This is the step size at which the discretized noise-free gradient descent process becomes unstable for quadratic costs. (We define λ max ≡ max k Λ kk analogous to λ min .)"
